From debdaa6bbc7caa5eacbe8137e090823037224e7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(worker): debug-mode integrity check of the display
 carve-out weights

The embedding and output-head weights in the GB10 display carve-out are
written once, by the copy after loading. Nothing else can reach them: the
DRM file and buffer handle close after the dma-buf export, the dma-buf stays
private to the worker, and the scanout heap never hands out a range in use.
A driver fault that reused that memory would still make the model serve
wrong embeddings or logits without any error.

For debugging such a fault, SPARK3_DISPLAY_CARVEOUT_CHECK_SECONDS above 0
turns on an integrity check. Unset or 0, the production setting, it adds no
work: one test at load time and nothing in the serving loop. When on, the
worker checksums every moved weight (the sum of its 32-bit words in 64 bits,
so any flipped bit changes it) before and after the copy and refuses to
start if they differ; after graph capture a background thread re-checks
them on a side stream at that interval, about 20 ms for the 842.5 MiB of a
DS4.1 rank. The sum runs in 16 MiB chunks: a 64-bit sum casts its input
first, and over a whole table the check stream's pool would keep 842 MiB. A
mismatch, or a check that cannot run, is logged and kills the worker, which
stops the engine.
